Find the game data on iOS when it was downloaded.

The macOS path handling already does the right thing on iOS and needs no
change: the writable directories come from Foundation
(~/Library/Application Support/0ad and ~/Library/Caches/0ad, which on iOS are
inside the app container), and RootData() returns the bundle's resources when
the executable is a bundle -- on iOS it always is.

The one thing that does not work is data *in* the bundle. An iOS bundle is read
only and signed, and 3.5 GB of game data cannot go in it -- the same reason the
Android port downloads the data on first start (patch 0010 there). So RootData()
looks next to the rest of the writable data first, and only falls back to the
bundle.

Keeping the bundle as the fallback is deliberate: it is what makes a
self-contained build possible, and that is how the smoke test runs, with the few
megabytes of the "mod" mod inside the bundle and no download at all.

--- source/ps/GameSetup/Paths.cpp
+++ source/ps/GameSetup/Paths.cpp
@@ -42,6 +42,28 @@
 #endif
 
 
+#if OS_IOS
+/**
+ * Where the game data ends up when it was downloaded rather than shipped.
+ *
+ * An iOS app bundle is read only and signed, so the 3.5 GB of game data cannot
+ * live in it -- the same reason the Android port downloads it on first start.
+ * The download goes next to the rest of the writable data, below
+ * ~/Library/Application Support/0ad (inside the app container), and this is
+ * where RootData() looks first. If it is not there, the bundle is used, which
+ * keeps a self-contained build possible: that is how the smoke test runs, with
+ * just the few MB of the "mod" mod inside the bundle.
+ */
+static OsPath IosDownloadedData()
+{
+	const std::string appSupport = osx_GetAppSupportPath();
+	if (appSupport.empty())
+		return OsPath();
+	return OsPath(appSupport) / "0ad" / "data"/"";
+}
+#endif
+
+
 Paths::Paths(const CmdLineArgs& args)
 {
 	m_root = Root(args.GetArg0());
@@ -202,6 +224,18 @@
 #ifdef INSTALLED_DATADIR
 	return OsPath(STRINGIZE(INSTALLED_DATADIR))/"";
 #else
+
+# if OS_IOS
+	// Downloaded data wins over whatever is in the bundle.
+	{
+		const OsPath downloaded = IosDownloadedData();
+		if (!downloaded.empty() && DirectoryExists(downloaded))
+		{
+			debug_printf("Using downloaded game data\n");
+			return downloaded;
+		}
+	}
+# endif
 
 # if OS_MACOSX
 	if (osx_IsAppBundleValid())
